  <h1>Common FMLA Misconceptions</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Riley Chen (RC), Employment &amp; Civil Rights Practice Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>&ldquo;FMLA applies to all employers.&rdquo;</h2><p>No. FMLA covers only employers with 50 or more employees within 75 miles of the work site. Employees of small businesses are not protected. Many states have state family leave laws (California CFRA, New York PFL) that cover smaller employers &mdash; check your state law.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;FMLA leave must be paid.&rdquo;</h2><p>FMLA provides unpaid, job-protected leave. The employer may require (or the employee may choose) to substitute accrued paid leave (vacation, sick time, PTO) for FMLA leave &mdash; but FMLA itself does not require pay. Many states have paid family leave programs (California, New York, New Jersey, Massachusetts, Washington) that run concurrently.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;Intermittent leave can&rsquo;t be used for chronic conditions.&rdquo;</h2><p>Incorrect. Intermittent FMLA leave is specifically available for chronic conditions requiring periodic treatment (migraines, Crohn&rsquo;s disease, depression, diabetes). Even single-day absences qualify if they are medically necessary. The employer cannot punish these absences if they are FMLA-designated.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;I need to file with the EEOC before suing.&rdquo;</h2><p>No. FMLA claims do not require EEOC exhaustion. You can file directly in federal district court (within 2&ndash;3 years) or with the DOL Wage and Hour Division. This is a significant distinction from Title VII and ADEA, which require EEOC charges and right-to-sue letters.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;Employers always owe liquidated damages for violations.&rdquo;</h2><p>Not automatically. The employer can defeat liquidated damages by proving good faith and reasonable grounds for believing the conduct did not violate the FMLA. Courts sometimes accept this defense for administrative errors by smaller employers without dedicated HR professionals.</p>
